<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iranian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iran is a country and historical region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. It was the heartland of a long line of empires, including the Median, Achaemenid, Parthian and Sasanian empires, and later the Safavid, Afsharid and Zand states. It was also ruled by external dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iran.jpg" alt="Iran" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG file: SiBr4Designer: Hamid NadimiConstruction: ISIRI · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iran_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Iran has an area of 1,648,195 square kilometres, the sixth-largest country entirely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It is one of the world's most mountainous countries, consisting mostly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian Plateau</a>, with ranges such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> in the populous west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-damavand">Mount Damavand</a>, at 5,610 metres, is the highest point and the highest volcano in Asia. Lush Caspian Hyrcanian forests cover the north, while the east is mostly desert basins such as the Kavir and Lut deserts. The country lies in a seismically active area, where an earthquake of magnitude 7.0 or higher occurs on average once every ten years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hominins were present in Iran around 800,000 years ago, and agriculture appeared some 12,000 years ago; Ganj Dareh shows the earliest known domestication of goats, around 10,000 years ago. Susa, later the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, was first settled in 4400–4200 BCE, and Elam lasted from 3200 to 539 BCE. The earliest Iranian peoples began to arrive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 2nd millennium BCE. The Median dynasty ruled the earliest Iranian state, and in 612 BCE Cyaxares and the Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> destroyed Nineveh. Cyrus the Great then defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, the largest-ever Iranian state, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered the empire in the 4th century BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> arose in the 3rd century BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> succeeded it in the 3rd century CE. After the 7th-century Muslim conquest Iran underwent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a>, and during the Iranian Intermezzo Iranian Muslim dynasties ended Arab rule and revived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-language">Persian language</a>. Seljuk and Khwarazmian rule, Mongol conquests and the Timurid Renaissance followed. In the 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> re-established a unified Iranian state with Twelver Shia Islam as its official religion. Iran was a leading world power under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> but lost the status after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> took power in the 1790s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> founded the Pahlavi dynasty after ousting the last Qajar Shah in 1925. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a> of 1979 overthrew the monarchy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> became the first supreme leader of the Islamic Republic; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> invaded in 1980, starting an eight-year war. The supreme leader is head of state and commands the armed forces, while the president has limited power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, with about 9.4 million people, is the capital and largest city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> are estimated at 51% of the population and Azerbaijanis at 24% in a 2003 estimate, although ethnic composition is debated because no state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> count it. Iran has 30 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The treasure Nader Shah took from Delhi in 1739 was so large that he remitted taxes throughout <b>Iran</b> for three years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was organised in clans under chiefs, with a warrior elite whose wealth is visible in rich burials containing weapons, horse gear, gold ornaments and objects imported from Rome, <b>Iran</b> and Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture in the river valleys of Mesopotamia and the plains of Azerbaijan and western <b>Iran</b> produced most of the state’s revenue, collected as land tax and as the tamgha, a tax on trade and urban crafts inherited from the Mongol period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With both frontiers under blockade, Armenia has built economic ties, including a gas pipeline, with <b>Iran</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Red Army suppressed the revolt of February 1921, and the anti-Soviet rebellion of Garegin Nzhdeh in mountainous Zangezur was defeated, its leaders driven across the Araks River into <b>Iran</b> on 15 July 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>More than 20,000 cuneiform tablets from Kanesh document Assyrian merchants bringing tin from the east, probably from <b>Iran</b> and Central Asia, and textiles from Babylonia and Assur itself, and exchanging them for Anatolian silver and gold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
